(2024).\n\nadditive-combinatoricscayley-graphsfinite-abelian-groups\n\n## Progress summary\n\nRefresh Refreshed 1 month ago\n\nOpen\n\nThe conjecture has matching constructions and broad upper bounds, but its exact answer remains unproved outside one congruence class.\n\nA 2024 preprint formulates the largest-noncyclic-group conjecture for two-element $s$s-spanning sets: the proposed maximum is $2s^2$2 s 2 when $2s+1$2 s+1 is prime, and $2s^2+2s-(p^2-1)/2$2 s 2+2 s−(p 2−1)/2 otherwise. It records substantial partial results but no proof of the full statement.\n\nKnown results\n\n  * The conjectured values are attained by explicit groups and spanning sets, so they are established as lower bounds (Theorem 1.7, 2024).\n  * Every noncyclic group with such a spanning set satisfies $|G|\\leq 2s^2+2s-4$∣G∣≤2 s 2+2 s−4.\n  * For $s$s-regular rank-$2$2 groups, the same upper bound holds; equality is characterized by $\\mathbb{Z}_3\\times\\mathbb{Z}_{(2s^2+2s-4)/3}$Z 3​×Z(2 s 2+2 s−4)/3​ when $s\\equiv1\\pmod3$s≡1(mod 3) (Theorem 3.3, 2024).\n  * Groups of the form $\\mathbb{Z}_2\\times\\mathbb{Z}_{2k}$Z 2​×Z 2 k​ satisfy the upper bound $2s^2$2 s 2.\n\nCurrent status (as of August 2026): the conjectured lower bounds and general upper bound are known, but the exact noncyclic maximum remains open for $s\\not\\equiv1\\pmod3$s≡1(mod 3); no proof or counterexample was found.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPapers from 2026-10-14 - arXiv Combinatorics (https://www.arxiv.symmetricfunctions.com/date/2026-10-14)\nciteturn7search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3 weeks ago; Crawled: yesterday; 2026-10-14 ... Finally, we prove that our refinement is symmetric by expressing it in terms of the Theta operators appearing in the Theta coinvariant Conjecture and the modified Macdonald symmetric functions.\n\n2026-10-14\n\nZiwei Lu, Yu Sun, Shenglin Zhou\n\n# • INFO • BIB • PDF • ARXIV •\n\nReduction for flag-transitive symmetric 2-designs with gcd(k, λ) = p\n\nWe study symmetric 2-(v, k, λ) designs admitting a flag-transitive point-primitive subgroup G of automorphisms, where gcd(k, λ) = p and p is a prime. In this paper, we show that G is of affine or almost simple type.\n\n2026-10-14\n\nHsin-Chieh Liao, Hai Zhu\n\n# • INFO • BIB • PDF • ARXIV •\n\nBicolored Eulerian quasisymmetric functions, affine lengths, and Theta operators\n\nWe introduce a $q$-analogue of the bicolored Eulerian quasisymmetric functions by incorporating an additional grading arising from affine length, obtained by interpreting bicolored permutations as affine permutations. We also provide an intrinsic description of this refinement in terms of bicolored permutations by introducing notions of alignments and crossings, together with an explicit bijection identifying these statistics with the alignments and crossings in the existing literature. Finally, we prove that our refinement is symmetric by expressing it in terms of the Theta operators appearing in the Theta coinvariant Conjecture and the modified Macdonald symmetric functions. Assuming the Theta coinvariant conjecture implies the Schur-positivity of our refinement.\n\naffine permutation coinvariant eulerian quasisymmetric function macdonald polynomial quasisymmetric function symmetric function theta operator permutation\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAlgebra & Number Theory vol. 18 (2024), no. 5 (https://msp.org/ant/2024/18-5/ant-v18-n5-s.pdf)\nciteturn7search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.6 years ago; Crawled: 6 months ago; 908  Gal Porat ... Higher locally analytic vectors. ... **Proposition 2.5.** (i) *There is a spectral sequence*\n908  Gal Porat\n\nFurthermore, if we fix \\(G\\) and consider small open subgroups \\(G' \\subset G\\), we may choose \\(V_\\ell(G') \\subset C^{an}(G', \\mathbb{Q}_p)\\) at once for all \\(G'\\) in such a way that the natural map \\(C^{an}(G, \\mathbb{Q}_p) \\to C^{an}(G', \\mathbb{Q}_p)